WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.880
Hi everyone, i'm Jacopo, Simone's friend,&nbsp;
the guy who made the previous videos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.080
I'm behind the editing and scripting of some&nbsp;
of them, for example the one about Colombia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.440
The idea behind this project is to alternate&nbsp;
our voices so in the future you will often hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:22.720
my broken English. But let's start.Our story&nbsp;
begins on April 17, 1895. Before this date,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.680
Taiwan had never been under total domination&nbsp;
of a single power. For the sake of briefness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.760
we’ll start off with the end of the nineteenth&nbsp;
century. With the treaty of shimonoseki in 1895,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:40.160
at the end of the first Sino-Japanese war, Formosa&nbsp;
(as Taiwan was called) and the Penghu islands were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.280
given in concession to Japan. Japan was very&nbsp;
interested in these islands, as he wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.640
affirm its position as a colonizing country. So,&nbsp;
in reaction to what many saw as a betrayal by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:57.120
China, some nobles loyal to the Ching Dynasty set&nbsp;
up the so-called Republic of Taiwan, to resist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.640
without success, to the new rulers. This episode&nbsp;
will provide one of the arguments used by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.560
current independentists against the prospect of&nbsp;
a new unification with mainland China. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.320
with the advent of the Land of the Rising Sun any&nbsp;
connection with China was interrupted to make room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:19.440
for the new demands and political objectives&nbsp;
of the emperor. The new occupants presented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:25.360
themselves as the benefactors who would raise the&nbsp;
cultural level of a Taiwan that according to them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:31.360
was in urgent need of reform. In a few decades&nbsp;
Japanese rule was instrumental in making Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:38.080
a modern colony in every aspect. And on this today&nbsp;
most Taiwanese agree: Japanese imperialism is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:44.240
longer seen as a dark parenthesis to be forgotten&nbsp;
but a fundamental stage in the country's progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.920
which gave it a separate status from both the&nbsp;
expanding Japanese empire and mainland China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:55.920
In fact, infrastructures were improved, public&nbsp;
hospitals were built, the first paved roads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.680
bridges, big power plants and even a railway&nbsp;
line. It was an all-round modernization which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:07.400
also interested the rural economy, with the birth&nbsp;
of the first chemical industries to refine sugar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:13.280
cane into sweetener and facilitate the export on&nbsp;
the Japanese market. After the Second World War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:19.920
the defeat of Japan meant that Taiwan, as a&nbsp;
result of the Cairo Declaration of November 1943,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:25.360
returned under Chinese sovereignty and precisely&nbsp;
under the control of the Republic of China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:30.240
which was none other than the Kuomintang&nbsp;
Nationalist Party (KMT), led by Chiang Jieshi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.760
who was appointed as trustee of the island on&nbsp;
behalf of the allied powers. The Nationalists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.800
immediately sought tight control over the&nbsp;
economic and political life of the island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.560
due to fear of Japanese influence, of the&nbsp;
pressures of reconstruction, and of the civil war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.400
on the mainland that opposed them to the Communist&nbsp;
led by Mao Zedong. The central government relied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.680
on a provincial administration composed almost&nbsp;
exclusively of mainland personnel, while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:03.040
Taiwanese tried to keep their autonomy with a&nbsp;
self-government, a sort of compromise between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:10.080
formal independence and complete submission.&nbsp;
From late 1945 to mid-1946, the nationalists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.560
increased tensions between the provincial&nbsp;
administration and the local population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:20.640
To many, China appeared chaotic and backward, as&nbsp;
a potential drain on the island's resources and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.760
threat to its stability, positioning itself&nbsp;
in the eyes of residents as a new but less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:31.040
competent colonial ruler. Reintegration&nbsp;
to Mainland China meant recolonization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:37.600
something the Taiwanese wanted to avoid at all&nbsp;
costs. In early 1947, tensions between state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:44.400
and society exploded in what became known as the&nbsp;
February 28 incident, triggered by the escape of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:49.760
a cigarette smuggler that caused police officers,&nbsp;
who were in hot pursuit, to fire shots to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.560
through the crowd. Protests in the following&nbsp;
days would allow the Taiwanese to quickly wrest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.360
control of the island from an ill-prepared&nbsp;
provincial administration. But not for long!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:05.200
Because after just one week, a military contingent&nbsp;
sent from the mainland crushed all opposition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.720
and massacred thousands of inhabitants. From that&nbsp;
moment began the long period of the so-called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:18.160
white terror (which lasted 38 years), during which&nbsp;
the Kmt tried to suppress even in blood any form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:24.880
of political dissent, arriving to imprison and&nbsp;
execute thousands of Taiwanese. In December 1949,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.320
at the end of the Chinese Civil War, what&nbsp;
was left of the defeated Nationalist Party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:36.480
retreated to Taiwan, thanks to US intervention&nbsp;
that prevented Communist from winning the race.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:41.920
Everything seemed to be turning for the worst, but&nbsp;
the government was able to promote a development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:49.360
that today is known as the "Taiwanese miracle".&nbsp;
Between 1960 and 1970, thanks to measures such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:55.520
rent reduction, expansion of education, land&nbsp;
reform, the introduction of a stock market and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.960
banks that encouraged foreign investments in&nbsp;
Taiwanese companies, the value of imports and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:06.080
exports increased dramatically and the average&nbsp;
annual growth in gross national product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:13.760
registered a 9.7 percent , with a per capita&nbsp;
gdp growth rate of 6.6 percent. Unlike the dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:20.560
period of the 1950s, when income distribution in&nbsp;
Taiwan was much like that of Mexico or Brazil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:27.840
characterized by a wide gap between the poorest&nbsp;
and richest citizens, by 1970 Taiwan's income&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:33.840
distribution was among the most equitable in the&nbsp;
world, better even than that of the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.160
It’s not surprising that foreign countries&nbsp;
found in Taiwan a valuable resource:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.920
a skilled, well-educated and highly motivated&nbsp;
workforce who saw in these measures a unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.200
opportunity to give a better life to their line of&nbsp;
succession. With this in mind, the authoritarian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:54.720
regime had little to fear that at some point the&nbsp;
residents would revolt, and on the off chance that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.800
this would happen, law enforcement would promptly&nbsp;
be used to suppress any dissent. This situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:07.120
of unconscious submission, let's call it that,&nbsp;
would only taper off from the 1980s onwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:13.120
when the middle class, which had grown thanks to&nbsp;
economic development, began to exert more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.720
pressure on the state for a democratic reform. In&nbsp;
fact, the big problem was that only a minority of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:24.560
the population participated in political life,&nbsp;
precisely 15%, corresponding to the military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:30.320
and civilian elite who came from mainland China&nbsp;
together with Chiang, and established themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:35.920
at the top of all aspects of society, again&nbsp;
confining the locals to a secondary status.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:42.320
If we step back a few years, we realize that&nbsp;
stability had begun to falter in 1971 when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:48.000
Republic of China lost its seat at the United&nbsp;
Nations to the People's Republic of China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:54.240
and so most states recognized the latter as the&nbsp;
sole legitimate representative of all of China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.440
And this, making a big leap into the present&nbsp;
day, brings us to the heated dispute between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:04.880
Taiwan and mainland China. The fundamental&nbsp;
question here is whether Taiwan, the Penghu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:10.880
Shinmen and Mazu islands should remain part of&nbsp;
the territory of the autonomous Republic of China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:16.800
or be unified with the territories now ruled by&nbsp;
the People's Republic of China. It all depends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:22.560
on the answer to the following question: is it&nbsp;
legitimate to recognize the Republic of China as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:28.160
a sovereign state? You need to know that Taiwan&nbsp;
is strongly penalized on an international level:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:34.800
we are dealing with a de facto state that is not&nbsp;
recognized as independent neither by the PRC nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.720
by the other permanent members of the UN Security&nbsp;
Council and that on paper maintains diplomatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:47.200
relations with the only 14 states that recognize&nbsp;
it. Beijing, for its part, considers it a Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:52.000
province temporarily out of its control, the&nbsp;
result of the dynamics of the Cold War, when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:58.320
United States prevented Mao from annihilating the&nbsp;
nationalists, a fact that allowed them to migrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.760
to the island and keep the Republic of China&nbsp;
alive. In order to stabilize relations between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:09.040
the two sides of the strait without renouncing&nbsp;
the reunification of the Chinese territory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:15.200
Beijing has imposed adherence to the principle of&nbsp;
the existence of a single China (the so-called one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:21.600
China policy). In practice, any state wishing&nbsp;
to establish or maintain relations with the PRC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:27.680
must recognize China with Beijing as the only&nbsp;
existing China and therefore deny sovereignty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:33.040
to Taiwan and not have official diplomatic&nbsp;
relations with it. Adhering to this request,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:40.320
the vast majority of countries, including the EU&nbsp;
member states, maintain very close but unofficial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:47.040
relations with Taiwan and therefore in many cases&nbsp;
cannot offer Taiwanese institutions or businessmen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:52.480
the same conditions normally applied to others.&nbsp;
On the opposìte front, Taipei authorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:58.720
claim instead to represent a fully sovereign&nbsp;
government; mind you, however that since 1949,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:04.160
when the Constitution of the Republic of China&nbsp;
came into force, Formosa adhered to the "One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:08.960
China principle", claiming sovereignty over&nbsp;
the territory now governed by the People's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:15.280
Republic of China along with Outer Mongolia.&nbsp;
But in 1991, after a series of revisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:20.880
the Kuomintang reduced its claims, recognizing&nbsp;
that its powers were limited only to the areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:25.760
under its control. A further rapprochement&nbsp;
with the political trajectory of the PRC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:32.960
was then the adherence to the so-called "1992&nbsp;
Consensus", whereby Beijing and Taipei agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:39.440
that there was only one China but agreed to have&nbsp;
different interpretations of what said China was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:45.040
whether the PRC or the Republic of China. More&nbsp;
recently, the fear that Taiwan could fall into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:50.240
Beijing's orbit has triggered a mobilization&nbsp;
of Taiwan's civil society, starting with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:57.280
more than 3 million young citizens between the&nbsp;
ages of 15 and 25. See experiences such as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:03.120
"Sunflower Movement," which in March 2014&nbsp;
marched over five hundred thousand people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.920
leading to the occupation of Parliament and,&nbsp;
briefly, the seat of government. This is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:14.560
result of widespread discontent arising from some&nbsp;
important phenomena such as: social inequities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.920
rising unemployment, difficulty in finding&nbsp;
a suitable job after studies and especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:25.840
the ecological degradation that plagues several&nbsp;
areas of the island. Regarding this last aspect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:32.080
of crucial importance for environmentalists&nbsp;
were the terrible earthquake of 2011 in Japan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:37.280
and the subsequent tsunami that caused the&nbsp;
accident at the Fukushima nuclear plant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:43.440
Events to which Taiwanese public opinion and media&nbsp;
have given great prominence, given that the island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:49.920
of Formosa is also located in a highly seismic&nbsp;
zone and has at least 4 nuclear plants, 3 of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:55.360
are located in the municipal area of the capital&nbsp;
Taipei. In any case, disrupting the status quo in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:02.240
terms of foreign policy has been the rise of the&nbsp;
Democratic Progressive Party led since 2016 by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:09.360
first female president Sai Ying-Wèn. Her approach&nbsp;
and willingness to reshape the 1992 Consensus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:15.360
were seen by Beijing as favorable to a process of&nbsp;
independence and generated an immediate reaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:22.880
as in 2017 the southern Chinese border hosted&nbsp;
at least 1,500 missiles aimed at the island.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:28.160
However, Taiwan has those who protect it.&nbsp;
With the signing of the Taiwan Relation Act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:33.680
in 1979, in fact, Washington committed&nbsp;
to defend the sovereignty of the island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:39.120
providing constant military supplies that would&nbsp;
act as a deterrent for a possible clash. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:44.880
is because the US has always considered Formosa&nbsp;
a fundamental strategic junction in the South&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:50.800
China Sea. But how is it possible that the United&nbsp;
States has official relations with both Beijing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:57.120
and Taipei? Didn't we say that one excludes the&nbsp;
other? Well, yes in theory, but in practice this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:02.880
is possible thanks to a series of interpretative&nbsp;
devices of the agreement with which the Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:10.080
have established diplomatic relations with the&nbsp;
PRC back in 1979. In fact, the PRC continues to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:16.160
believe that by adhering to that document the&nbsp;
Americans have also implicitly accepted the PRC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:21.520
as China alone. But Washington doesn’t think&nbsp;
so, since it has never expressly followed this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:28.000
interpretation. However, the U.S. have managed to&nbsp;
not disappoint neither Mainland China nor Taiwan:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:33.360
on the one hand they have never expressly&nbsp;
supported Taiwanese independence aspirations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:39.040
meeting the favor of Beijing and on the other&nbsp;
hand they have continued to pursue their policy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:44.880
of deterrence, maintaining close cooperation with&nbsp;
Taipei. The prospect of a conflict in the Strait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:50.560
is not ruled out. Although not very convenient&nbsp;
for both actors, given the huge volume of trade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:55.680
between the Taiwanese industries in Chinese&nbsp;
territory, but if this were to happen, the fate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.680 --> 00:13:01.040
of Taiwan would be quite predictable, assuming&nbsp;
there is no interference from outside. Let’s put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:09.120
it in this way: in 2017 Chinese military spending&nbsp;
was about $145 billion and active troops amounted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:14.800
to more than eight hundred thousands, compared&nbsp;
to only one hundred thirty thousand Taiwanese.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:20.080
Not to mention that Taiwan's air force doesn't&nbsp;
seem to be able to compete with China's new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:25.440
Chengdu J-20 stealth fighters. The navy, on&nbsp;
the other hand, is the real flagship of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:31.200
Taiwanese army, but it has constant difficulty in&nbsp;
modernizing its technologies for certain sectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:37.040
due to the blockades that Beijing imposes on&nbsp;
military companies. In 2021, for the Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:43.920
Communist Party Centenary, Beijing has again put&nbsp;
pressure on Taipei, with at least 600 air raids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:50.000
of which 150 in the first week of October&nbsp;
alone. Saying that the invasion is imminent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:54.480
is too difficult to predict. According to&nbsp;
the words of the Taiwanese defense minister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.480 --> 00:14:00.960
Beijing would have the capabilities to annex the&nbsp;
island by 2025, but not to undermine the sector&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:06.960
of essential goods called semiconductors, of which&nbsp;
Taiwan is one of the largest producers. For such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:12.000
reason, China may think twice before excluding&nbsp;
the diplomatic route. And we're done today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:17.520
I hope I gave you a clear idea of Taiwan’s recent&nbsp;
history, that’s what mattered. We'll definitely be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:23.120
hearing about Taiwan again and again in the coming&nbsp;
months. See you in the next video. Bye!

